# Targeted SELinux allow rule for the vscodium-box NVIDIA GPU container.
#
# Why this exists: the host's /dev/nvidia* nodes (nvidiactl, nvidia0,
# nvidia-uvm, nvidia-uvm-tools, nvidia-modeset) carry the SELinux type
# xserver_misc_device_t (systemd-udev labels them that way). The container's
# process domain (container_t) is allowed dri_device_t (that is why passing
# /dev/dri for plain rendering works out of the box) but is NOT allowed
# xserver_misc_device_t, so NVML and CUDA inside the box fail with
# "Failed to initialize NVML: Insufficient Permissions" even though every
# device node is present and world-accessible (mode 0666, per the CDI spec).
#
# This module allows container_t to open/read/write/ioctl the NVIDIA device
# nodes. It applies to any container on the host, not just vscodium-box - the
# same unavoidable trade-off as the Wayland connectto rule documented in
# README.md. It grants no capabilities and touches no other types.
#
# Built with `ns:=true` style module packing:
#   checkmodule -M -m -o vscodium-box-nvidia.mod vscodium-box-nvidia.te
#   semodule_package -o vscodium-box-nvidia.pp -m vscodium-box-nvidia.mod
#   sudo semodule -i vscodium-box-nvidia.pp
# install-vscodium.sh attempts the last step best-effort (see
# ensure_nvidia_selinux_module); remove with
#   sudo semodule -r vscodium-box-nvidia
module vscodium-box-nvidia 1.0;

require {
    type container_t;
    type xserver_misc_device_t;
    class chr_file { open read write ioctl getattr setattr lock map };
}

allow container_t xserver_misc_device_t:chr_file { open read write ioctl getattr setattr lock map };